    id: 357, 
    question: 'What is the difference between compiler and interpreter?', 
    answer: 'Compiler translates entire code to machine code before execution. Interpreter translates and executes line-by-line.\n\n🍜 COOKING ANALOGY:\nCOMPILER = Meal prep:\n- Prepare all dishes on Sunday\n- Just heat and eat during week\n- Faster meals but takes time upfront\n\nINTERPRETER = Cook on demand:\n- Make each meal fresh when needed\n- Slower but more flexible\n- Can change recipe anytime\n\nKEY DIFFERENCES:\n\n📄 COMPILER:\n✅ Converts entire program at once\n✅ Creates executable file (.exe)\n✅ Faster execution (optimized)\n❌ Slower compilation\n❌ Errors shown after full scan\n💻 Examples: C, C++, Java\n\n📝 INTERPRETER:\n✅ Translates line by line\n✅ No executable file\n✅ Easier debugging (stops at error)\n✅ Platform independent\n❌ Slower execution\n💻 Examples: Python, JavaScript\n\nCOMPILATION PROCESS:\n```\nSource Code (.c, .cpp)\n    ⬇️\n[Compiler]\n    ⬇️\nMachine Code (.exe)\n    ⬇️\nExecution\n```\n\nINTERPRETATION PROCESS:\n```\nSource Code (.py, .js)\n    ⬇️\n[Interpreter]\n    ⬇️\nDirect Execution\n(line by line)\n```\n\n🎯 WHEN TO USE WHAT:\n- Compiled (C++): Games, OS, performance-critical\n- Interpreted (Python): Scripts, rapid development, learning\n\n💡 HYBRID APPROACH (Java):\n1. Source code → Compiler → Bytecode\n2. Bytecode → JVM Interpreter → Execution\nBest of both worlds!', 
    category: 'Fresher CS Fundamentals', 
    difficulty: 'Medium', 
    tags: ['basics', 'concepts'] 
