    question: 'What is Git and why is it important?', 
    answer: 'Git is a version control system that tracks code changes, enables collaboration, and maintains project history.\n\n💾 SAVE GAME ANALOGY:\nGit = Save points in video games:\n- Commit = Save your progress\n- Branch = Try different strategies\n- Merge = Combine successful attempts\n- Revert = Go back to previous save\n\nCORE CONCEPTS:\n\n📂 REPOSITORY (Repo):\nProject folder with Git tracking\n- Local: On your computer\n- Remote: On GitHub/GitLab\n\n📦 COMMIT:\nSnapshot of code at a point in time\n```bash\ngit add .              # Stage changes\ngit commit -m "Added login feature"\n```\n\n🌳 BRANCH:\nIndependent line of development\n```bash\ngit branch feature-login  # Create branch\ngit checkout feature-login # Switch to it\n# Work on feature without affecting main code\n```\n\n🔀 MERGE:\nCombine branches\n```bash\ngit checkout main\ngit merge feature-login  # Bring changes to main\n```\n\nBASIC GIT WORKFLOW:\n```bash\n# 1. Initialize repository\ngit init\n\n# 2. Check status\ngit status\n\n# 3. Stage files\ngit add file.txt       # Specific file\ngit add .              # All files\n\n# 4. Commit changes\ngit commit -m "Your message"\n\n# 5. Push to remote (GitHub)\ngit push origin main\n\n# 6. Pull latest changes\ngit pull origin main\n```\n\n📊 REAL-WORLD SCENARIO:\nTeam of 3 developers:\n```bash\nAlice: Works on login (branch: feature-login)\nBob: Fixes bugs (branch: bugfix-payment)\nCharlie: Adds dashboard (branch: feature-dashboard)\n\n# Each works independently!\n# When done, merge to main branch\n# No code conflicts, clean collaboration\n```\n\nWHY GIT IS CRITICAL:\n✅ Track every change (who, what, when)\n✅ Revert mistakes instantly\n✅ Collaborate without conflicts\n✅ Experiment safely with branches\n✅ Code backup (GitHub = cloud)\n✅ Portfolio showcase (employers check GitHub!)\n\n👥 COLLABORATION:\n```bash\n# Fork: Copy someone\'s project\n# Clone: Download to your computer\n# Pull Request: Propose changes\n# Review: Team checks your code\n# Merge: Changes accepted!\n```\n\n⚠️ COMMON MISTAKES:\n- Not committing regularly\n- Poor commit messages ("fixed stuff")\n- Working directly on main branch\n- Not pulling before pushing\n\n✅ BEST PRACTICES:\n- Commit often with clear messages\n- Use branches for new features\n- Pull before starting work\n- Write descriptive commit messages\n- Learn basic commands first', 
